{
  "name": "Getting Started with Laundry Room Accessibility Modifications",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "If you're looking to make your laundry room more accessible, it's really about taking things one step at a time to create a space that truly works for you. First off, you'll want to carefully measure your room and any pathways leading to it. This helps identify where you might have tight squeezes or obstacles. Then, think about how you use the space: where are the reach or transfer barriers? Are controls too high, or is bending over a pain? Once you've got a good handle on that, you can start looking at accessible appliances and hardware that make daily tasks easier. This often means choosing front-loading machines and lever-style handles. After that, we can adjust shelving and counters to be at a comfortable height, swap out slippery flooring for something more secure, and even add safety features like easier-to-reach shut-offs. Remember, the goal is to make laundry less of a chore and more manageable, helping you maintain independence in your home. Sometimes, getting professional help is key, especially if you're dealing with electrical, plumbing, or any structural changes.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
